CLAIM           On every rest scaffold whose candidate graph is triangle-free, no bond forms at ANY of its states: B(t+1) is a subset of B(t) everywhere, exhaustively. On the scaffolds that do have triangles, states where a bond forms exist. The zero object count on the square scaffolds is this, not a coincidence.
WOULD OVERTURN  A single state on a triangle-free scaffold where a bond forms -- that kills the theorem outright. Or no forming state anywhere on a triangled scaffold, which would mean triangles are not what distinguishes the two families and the mechanism named here is the wrong one.
UNIVERSE        827648   (every state of every scaffold listed, each visited once; complete enumeration, no sampling and no horizon)
CONTROLS        5 declared, 3 negative, all as declared
VALUE           0 of 270,592 triangle-free states form a bond; all 2 triangled scaffolds do
DETAIL          the claim holds iff no triangle-free state forms and every triangled scaffold has one (HOLDS)

